  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1=0,$ find the remaining solutions.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=36{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+4x+1.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $36$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\u2193$ | $-9$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $36$ | $0$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(36{x}^{2}+4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $36{x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 36{x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}.$",
